 "_note": "What the numbers show: <b>S\u00e1nchez's velocity held</b> (95.2 \u2192 95.1 mph), but his bat-missing faded. CSW fell from 32.7% to 28.5%, K% from 28.6% to 23.7%, and HR/BF roughly doubled (1.7% \u2192 3.2%) over his last six starts. He finished 3.2 K's below what our model projected in those starts. He only passed his prior career high (202 IP) on Sep 25, so the innings-vs-career-high flag alone wouldn't have caught it. The CSW and whiff trend did. <b>Misiorowski's stuff did not wear down</b>: velocity was 100.3 in H1 and 100.4 in his last six. But his outings got shorter (6.2 \u2192 4.9 IP, 92 \u2192 78 pitches) after he blew past his 66-IP prior high in May, and K% dipped from 38.8% to 31.3%. He finished 19.3 K's under our projections over those six starts, the biggest miss of the three. That looks like workload management plus regression, not a velocity crash. <b>Schlittler kept going</b>: velocity rose (97.7 \u2192 98.3), spin rose, CSW rose (28.9% \u2192 32.2%) and K% rose (29.7% \u2192 34.3%), all while he more than doubled his prior high (73 IP). Across all 2026 starters, pitchers at 1.0\u20131.2\u00d7 their prior high struck out about 0.34 fewer than projected, vs 0.12\u20130.17 fewer in every other bucket (including >1.2\u00d7, where Schlittler-type arms live). That's a modest real effect, which is why workload is a feature and not a hard rule."
}